solution_steps:
We can use the sum and difference identities for sine to write
\begin{align*}
\sin(a+b) &= \sin a \cos b + \cos a \sin b = \frac{3}{4}, \\
\sin(a-b) &= \sin a \cos b - \cos a \sin b = \frac{1}{2}.
\end{align*}
Adding these equations, we get
\begin{align*}
2 \sin a \cos b &= \frac{3}{4} + \frac{1}{2} = \frac{5}{4},
\end{align*}
and subtracting these equations, we get
\begin{align*}
2 \cos a \sin b &= \frac{3}{4} - \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{4}.
\end{align*}
Dividing these two equations, we get
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sin a \cos b}{\cos a \sin b} &= \frac{\frac{5}{4}}{\frac{1}{4}} = \boxed{5}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: 5

