solution_steps:
To rationalize the denominator, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, which is $\sqrt[3]{3^2} + \sqrt[3]{3} \cdot \sqrt[3]{2} + \sqrt[3]{2^2}$.
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{\sqrt[3]{3} - \sqrt[3]{2}} &= \frac{1 \cdot (\sqrt[3]{3^2} + \sqrt[3]{3} \cdot \sqrt[3]{2} + \sqrt[3]{2^2})}{(\sqrt[3]{3} - \sqrt[3]{2}) \cdot (\sqrt[3]{3^2} + \sqrt[3]{3} \cdot \sqrt[3]{2} + \sqrt[3]{2^2})} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt[3]{3^2} + \sqrt[3]{3} \cdot \sqrt[3]{2} + \sqrt[3]{2^2}}{(\sqrt[3]{3})^3 - (\sqrt[3]{2})^3} \\
&= \frac{\sqrt[3]{9} + \sqrt[3]{6} + \sqrt[3]{4}}{3 - 2} \\
&= \sqrt[3]{9} + \sqrt[3]{6} + \sqrt[3]{4}
\end{align*}
The fraction is already in lowest terms, so $A + B + C + D = 9 + 6 + 4 + 1 = \boxed{20}$.The answer is: 20

